# enforce-invariants-structurally-not-procedurally

When a correctness constraint can be made structurally unviolable — through data-type design, infrastructure wiring, or explicit enumeration — prefer that over relying on discipline at runtime. This principle surfaced in three distinct forms: encoding co-occurring fields as atomic invariants in state machines so partial/inconsistent state cannot be represented; using real infrastructure so fake or stub paths cannot silently substitute for real ones; and enumerating all intended targets explicitly so coverage gaps are forced to be visible rather than silently absent. In each case, the structural approach eliminates an entire class of silent violation that a procedural check could only catch after the fact.

## Procedures
- When two or more fields/behaviors must always co-occur, model them as a single atomic unit in the type system rather than as separate fields guarded by runtime checks.
- Wire real implementations end-to-end (real infra, real async tasks that actually run) rather than inserting stubs or mocks that could silently diverge from real behavior.
- Enumerate all intended targets explicitly in code or configuration so that any gap (a missing target, an unprocessed item) produces a visible failure rather than a silent omission.

